    category: 'Vinyl Guide',
    headline: 'Vinyl flooring for professional kitchens',
    intro: 'The professional kitchen imposes some of the strictest requirements of any floor-covering installation: HACCP standards, mandatory anti-slip, resistance to thermal shock, grease and aggressive cleaning products. Here is how to choose.',
    sections: [
      { title: 'The standards and obligations', body: "Professional kitchens (restaurants, hotels, public bodies) are subject to HACCP regulations that require impermeable coverings, smooth or lightly textured, resistant to cleaning and disinfection products, and with no joints inaccessible to cleaning.\n\nAnti-slip is imposed by labour law for premises with a slip risk. The R classification of floors gives the anti-slip level: R10 for dry kitchens, R11 or R12 for wet and greasy zones. CB Sols selects R11-minimum approved products for all professional kitchens.\n\nChemical resistance is essential: concentrated alkaline cleaning products, chlorinated disinfectants, food grease: the floor must withstand daily cycles." },
      { title: 'The suitable types of vinyl floor', body: "Homogeneous vinyl is the most suitable for professional kitchens. Unlike heterogeneous vinyl (superimposed layers), it is made of a single layer of material running right to the surface. No top layer to wear, no possible delamination. Its lifespan is exceptional: 20 to 30 years in intensive use.\n\nThe Gerflor Mipolam (Granit, Unicolore) and Taralay products are the market benchmarks for professional kitchens. They offer the R10/R11 classification, chemical resistance and the required hygiene certifications.\n\nCoved skirtings are mandatory to prevent seepage under the floor. CB Sols forms coved skirtings (welded vinyl running 10 to 20 cm up the walls) for perfect watertightness." },
      { title: 'Heat welding: why it is mandatory', body: "In a professional kitchen, the sheets of vinyl floor must be heat-welded, never simply butted and glued. Heat welding creates a watertight junction between the sheets by melting a PVC weld cord of the same composition into the joint groove.\n\nWithout welding, the joints let liquids, grease and micro-organisms seep under the covering: guaranteed HACCP non-compliance and a source of rapid degradation of the floor and the substrate.\n\nCB Sols has professional heat-welding equipment and trains its fitters in this technique. All our professional-kitchen projects include the welding and watertight coved skirtings." },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Which floor for a restaurant kitchen?', a: 'Homogeneous vinyl, R11 minimum, heat-welded with coved skirtings. P2 minimum chemical-resistance rating. References: Gerflor Mipolam Granit, Gerflor Taralay Premium Contract. CB Sols provides a bespoke quote after a site visit.' },
      { q: 'Can you lay vinyl over tiles in a kitchen?', a: 'Yes, provided the tiling is perfectly flat (2 mm tolerance under a 2 m straightedge), in good condition, with no loose tiles and no proud joints. A surfacing levelling compound is often needed to erase the joint relief. CB Sols assesses this point when quoting.' },
      { q: 'How much does a professional kitchen floor cost?', a: 'A professional kitchen vinyl floor costs between 45 and 90 €/m² supplied and fitted, depending on product quality, area and the complexity of laying (coved skirtings, welding, furniture to move). CB Sols provides a free, detailed quote after a visit.' },
    ],
    relatedSolutions: ['sol-pvc-restaurant', 'sol-pvc-hotel'],
